0,"path":251,"stem":252},"What are Digital Twins?","\u002Fblog\u002Fwhat-are-digital-twins","blog\u002FWhat are Digital Twins",{"title":254,"path":255,"stem":256},"What makes Mixed Reality the next big thing?","\u002Fblog\u002Fwhat-makes-mixed-reality-the-next-big-thing","blog\u002FWhat makes Mixed Reality the next big thing",{"title":258,"path":259,"stem":260},"Why Game Engines?","\u002Fblog\u002Fwhy-game-engines","blog\u002FWhy Game Engines",{"title":262,"path":263,"stem":264},"Why we need User Experience Design?","\u002Fblog\u002Fwhy-we-need-user-experience-design","blog\u002FWhy we need User Experience Design",false,{"id":267,"title":154,"author":268,"body":272,"date":361,"description":362,"extension":363,"image":364,"meta":365,"minRead":366,"navigation":367,"path":155,"seo":368,"stem":156,"__hash__":369},"blog\u002Fblog\u002FProcesses for Better Software Volume 2.md",{"name":269,"avatar":270},"Aghil Jose",{"src":271,"alt":269},"\u002Fassets\u002Fimages\u002Fabout.webp",{"type":273,"value":274,"toc":352},"minimark",[275,279,282,287,294,297,301,307,310,314,320,323,327,333,336,340,346,349],[276,277,278],"p",{},"Every engineer\u002Fdeveloper has the right to do their work the right way, not\nnecessarily the easy way.",[276,280,281],{},"There are a lot of reasons a software fails to serve its intended purpose. But\nmost of them could be avoided if we implement the so called good practices\ninto the software development life-cycle.",[283,284,286],"h2",{"id":285},"_1-accurate-understanding-of-end-user-needs","1. Accurate understanding of end-user needs",[276,288,289],{},[290,291],"img",{"alt":292,"src":293},"correctFirst","\u002Fassets\u002Fimages\u002Fblog\u002F020920\u002FcorrectFirst.webp",[276,295,296],{},"No matter you are making software as a product or for a client, you have to\nhave a clear understanding of the end-user needs. Significant man hours have\ngone to waste and so have many applications built during it just by failing to\nunderstand the same. The end-user or client needs cannot usually be determined\nby one single meeting. It requires good research and understanding the problem\nstatement first-hand. Then and only then we could try and solve the problem\nmore efficiently.",[283,298,300],{"id":299},"_2-taking-it-slow","2. Taking it slow",[276,302,303],{},[290,304],{"alt":305,"src":306},"refactor","\u002Fassets\u002Fimages\u002Fblog\u002F020920\u002Frefactor.webp",[276,308,309],{},"“Slow and steady wins the race”. Sure, you could actually make the software\ndevelopment process much faster, but usually at the expense of compromising\nsome vital steps. The applications developed skipping these “non-essential\nsteps” might sooner or later become a big problem. It might be true that you\ncould save the time and resources while building it in the first place. But,\nwhen (not if) it comes back with bugs, it will cost you more of both.",[283,311,313],{"id":312},"_3-refactor","3. Refactor",[276,315,316],{},[290,317],{"alt":318,"src":319},"slow","\u002Fassets\u002Fimages\u002Fblog\u002F020920\u002Fslow.webp",[276,321,322],{},"There is usually the need to upgrade and maintain a software as time goes on.\nWhenever you see the need and have the chance do not hesitate to refactor the\ncode. Refactoring will usually help create a code base with increased\nreadability, smooth execution, less bugs and higher performance.",[283,324,326],{"id":325},"_4-make-code-correct-first-and-fast-second","4. Make code correct first and fast second",[276,328,329],{},[290,330],{"alt":331,"src":332},"userNeeds","\u002Fassets\u002Fimages\u002Fblog\u002F020920\u002FuserNeeds.webp",[276,334,335],{},"Your primary focus should always be achieving the desired functionality. It\ndoesn’t matter how better the design or how fast the response is, if it cannot\nproduce appropriate output, that software is useless and a liability.",[283,337,339],{"id":338},"_5-using-version-control","5. Using version control",[276,341,342],{},[290,343],{"alt":344,"src":345},"versionControl","\u002Fassets\u002Fimages\u002Fblog\u002F020920\u002FversionControl.webp",[276,347,348],{},"Do not wait for the software to grow beyond a certain size to incorporate\nversion control applications such as GitHub or bitbucket. It is always a good\npractice to create repositories from day one. Create branches and maintain it\nresponsibly. My method is to always keep at least three branches even for a\nsingle engineer. One for release, one with the latest stable version and one\nfor the working copy. Any method would be good as long as it helps to keep a\nbackup as well as a smooth workflow.",[276,350,351],{},"Disclaimer : The views and opinions expressed in the article belong solely to the\nauthor, and not necessarily to the author's employer, organisation, committee\nor other group or individual.",{"title":353,"searchDepth":354,"depth":354,"links":355},"",2,[356,357,358,359,360],{"id":285,"depth":354,"text":286},{"id":299,"depth":354,"text":300},{"id":312,"depth":354,"text":313},{"id":325,"depth":354,"text":326},{"id":338,"depth":354,"text":339},"2020-09-02","Best practices for software engineers continued.","md","\u002Fassets\u002Fimages\u002Fblog\u002F020920\u002Fprocess2Title.webp",{},3,true,{"title":154,"description":362},"rx0AS0m72dygKZsz8bQJdweR7KMPsSGnAEZ1YFJoPKw",[371,374],{"path":67,"title":66,"description":372,"date":373},"A brief explanation of the need and use of design patterns.","2020-09-07",{"path":151,"title":150,"description":375,"date":376},"Best practices for Software Engineers.","2020-08-24",1790338182927]